course: dictionary
lesson: 5827
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A mailbox is a box where letters are delivered, or a public box where you post letters. Context tells you which kind is meant. In «Grandpa wins every year with a rubber snake in the mailbox», the mailbox is a real box you could put a snake in.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Mailbox can also mean the place on a computer where your emails arrive. If the text talks about stamps and letters, it is the box outside. If it talks about screens, passwords or emails, it is the computer place.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably found the right meaning. If it sounds silly, look again at the words around it.
fact: A mailbox is a box where letters are delivered, or a public box where you post letters.
fact: In the UK, people usually say postbox or letterbox for this kind of box.
fact: An email mailbox is the place on a computer where your emails arrive.
fact: «Put the letter in the mailbox» means post it so it can be delivered.
fact: Be careful with emails from strangers in your mailbox, and ask a trusted adult before you open anything you do not recognise.
exercise: «Grandpa wins every year with a rubber snake in the mailbox.» What is a mailbox in this sentence? | *A box outside a house where things can be kept | A bicycle with a bell | A type of cake | A river that runs past the house
exercise: Ella says, «My email mailbox is full.» What does mailbox mean here? | A wooden crate for apples | *The place on a computer where emails arrive | A postbox on the corner of the street | A parcel sent by train
exercise: Which word would a British child use for a box outside a house where letters are delivered? | Mailbag | Envelope | Stamp | *Letterbox
test: «Put the letter in the mailbox» means what? | Tear the letter up and throw it away | *Post the letter so it can be delivered | Stick the letter on a wall | Read the letter out loud
test: Someone asks, «Should I open emails from strangers in my mailbox?» What is the safe answer? | *No, be careful and ask a trusted adult first | Yes, always open them straight away | Yes, if they have pictures | No, because mailboxes are made of wood
exam: In which sentence does mailbox mean a box outside a house? | My mailbox has a password | The mailbox on my laptop is full | The emails in my mailbox were spam | *She posted the card in the mailbox on the corner
